35,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"It is a pleasure to see you all again. I have spent some time reflecting on the records, and they have given me much to think about regarding how we value different types of research.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"The data reveals a concerning gap. While there is an abundance of applicants for modelling, we have lost most of our trained phenotyping technicians to industry, and senior analysts are currently seconded abroad.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"My findings are equally concerning. While some facilities are stable, the glasshouses are fully booked, and the spectroscopy suite suffered significant downtime due to lapsed contracts and staffing shortages. Our infrastructure is fragile.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems we are facing a curious paradox. While the agency remains generous with computing and glasshouse subsidies, the actual cost of physical logistics and animal work has climbed significantly. The ""standard"" grant is shrinking in real terms.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Midori,,"I've found that projects relying on live breeding colonies are much more prone to schedule overruns than computational ones. On a brighter note, the national repository's turnaround time has improved significantly.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"@Midori, it is interesting that we found similar patterns regarding breeding colonies. It seems the quiet, steady work often proves more reliable than the bold promises that sometimes falter.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Victoria, your findings on the spectroscopy suite align with my concerns. If the technical staff are gone and the equipment is failing, any proposal relying on high-end phenotyping is fundamentally unrealistic, regardless of the budget.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Ryuji, exactly. It is not just about the budget, but the actual capacity to execute. If the infrastructure is this fragile, we must prioritize proposals that are resilient to these systemic failures.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"Precisely. If we award a grant to a project that requires a functioning spectroscopy suite or a fleet of vehicles, we are essentially gambling with the agency's funds. Resilience is indeed the most logical metric here.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Midori,,"I agree with you all. It sounds like we should be very cautious about projects that depend on those fragile systems. I'm glad the repository is faster, but the breeding colony risks are worrying.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Shizue,,"It is heartening to see us all reaching a common understanding. Perhaps we should look for a project that is humble in its needs but enduring in its value, rather than one that risks everything on fragile systems.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,I agree. We must prioritize a proposal that aligns with the current reality of our workforce and infrastructure. A project that requires specialized technicians who no longer exist is a project destined for failure.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Victoria,,I agree. We cannot ignore the reality of the current bottlenecks. A proposal that assumes a seamless operational environment is a fantasy; we need a project that can succeed despite these constraints.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,Indeed. It would be a charming tragedy to fund a masterpiece that cannot be painted because the brushes are missing. We require a candidate whose methodology is as robust as their hypothesis.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Midori,,I completely agree. It feels like we're all seeing the same picture now. We should look for a candidate whose plan is realistic and won't be derailed by these external pressures.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Shizue,,I believe we are ready to see the candidates. Let us welcome them with kindness and judge their proposals with the wisdom we have gathered today. I am eager to see who among them fits our needs.,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Ryuji,,I agree. We have a clear set of criteria now. I will be looking for a candidate whose project is sustainable given the current staffing shortages and infrastructure limitations. Let us see the proposals.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Victoria,,"I concur. We have a shared understanding of the risks. I will be evaluating the candidates based on how their resource requirements align with the actual, current capacity of our shared services.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"I shall be looking for a candidate who has accounted for the shifting financial landscape. A proposal that ignores the rising cost of logistics is merely a wish list, not a plan. I am ready.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Midori,,I'm ready too. I'll be keeping a close eye on those breeding colony risks and hoping for a project that can move forward smoothly without getting stuck in those bottlenecks. Let's see the candidates!,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
